{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And why have you brought, etc.</b> A second complaint: why did they bring them by way of the wilderness, which is a place of danger of death by thirst, when they should have taken them up by a route other than through the wilderness? And the intention here is: since there is no power in Moses' hand to give them water in the wilderness, on whom did he rely to bring them through there?" "And their saying <b>and why have you brought us up, etc.</b> — meaning, if you answer us that there is no assured route to bring you through except by way of the wilderness, or that necessity compelled the matter that you travel through the wilderness — therefore they complained and said: if so, you should not have brought us up from Egypt, since it was inevitable to bring us to this evil place where there is danger, which is not a place of seed, etc., as they said, of blessed memory (Ḥullin 88b): a wilderness produces no vegetation." "And they said <b>to the place</b> with the definite article, to say that the evil qualities of the wilderness are well known — that it is not a place of, etc. <b>and there is no water to drink</b> — meaning, even to drink, which is a vital necessity, and you are unable to provide us with water. And Moses had no answer in his mouth, and he stood in prayer before HaShem — he and Aaron — as it is written: ״וַיִּפְּלוּ עַל פְּנֵיהֶם״."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:08.429386019Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}